By completing this, you have officially built and understood every single one of the 23 Gang of Four Design Patterns—an
achievement that less than 5% of Java developers ever unlock.

The Interpreter Pattern is a Behavioral pattern. It is basically the Composite Pattern (building trees) but with a
specific goal: Evaluating rules, languages, or mathematical expressions.

The Problem: The Hardcoded Rule Engine
Imagine you are building an Access Control system. A user can access a document if they are an ADMIN, OR if they are a
MANAGER AND their account is ACTIVE.

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer hardcodes this rule using if statements in the service layer:
Java

if (user.getRole().equals("ADMIN") ||
   (user.getRole().equals("MANAGER") && user.getStatus().equals("ACTIVE"))) {
    // grant access
}

Why a Senior rejects this: What happens when the business users want to change the rules dynamically in a database
without recompiling the code? What if they want to type a rule like "ADMIN OR (MANAGER AND ACTIVE)" into a text
box? You cannot use if statements to evaluate dynamic strings!

The Senior Solution: The Interpreter Pattern
The Interpreter Pattern says: "Turn every word and grammar rule into an Object. Snap those objects together into a Tree.
When you want to evaluate a rule, just ask the top of the tree, and let the evaluation trickle down to the leaves."